ted local survey","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":18},{"id":"ae2265a4-7ef4-4481-80d3-4fd336189193","name":"Saptashrungi Devi Temple, Vani","deity":"Saptashrungi Nivasini (Durga)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Shakti Peetha"],"country":"India","state":"Maharashtra","district":"Nashik","town":"Vani","latitude":20.0333,"longitude":73.7833,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":"Temple-trust guesthouse, on site","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0,"nearest_river_name":"Godavari River","river_distance_km":2.1,"best_season_notes":"Sep–Oct for Navaratri; otherwise Oct–Feb for the climb up to the hillside shrine.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T13:20:34.20482+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"The Goddess — an 18-armed image carved directly into the cliff rock and coated in sindoor — is revered as one of Maharashtra's three-and-a-half Shaktipeethas (with Mahalakshmi-Kolhapur, Tulja Bhavani-Tuljapur and Renuka-Mahur), and among the 51 Shaktipeethas of the subcontinent.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"श्री सप्तशृंगी देवी मंदिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":"Per a moderate-authority panchang site, not an official schedule: darshan 6am–9pm; Kakad Aarti 5:30–6:30am; Panchamrit Mahapuja 7–8am; Mahanaivedya Aarti 12–1pm; Sandhya Aarti 7–8pm.","accessibility_notes":"Reached by more than 500 stone steps cut into the hillside (472 built in 1710, more added in 1768); a ropeway covering the same climb is also available.","nearest_airport_name":"Ojhar / Nashik International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":70,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Nashik Road railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":75,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Saptashrungi)","architecture_style":"Not a constructed temple in the conventional sense — a natural rock-cut cave shrine on a Sahyadri cliff face at about 1,230 m elevation","construction_century":null},{"id":"ae234131-6076-481f-bb30-bbf47892d5b6","name":"Gangamma Temple, Dhenuvakonda","deity":"Gangamma","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Andhra Pradesh","district":"Prakasam","town":"Doddavaram","latitude":15.70209,"longitude":79.95614,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Gundlakamma River","river_distance_km":1.8,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:36.374917+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple to the goddess Gangamma on the outskirts of Dhenuva Konda in Prakasam district, Andhra Pradesh. Sunday is the preferred day for worship. The Yadava community, for whom she is a powerful figure, holds a jatara on the April full moon, when the temple is lit up and devotees pray all day and night.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Vijayawada Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":129.1,"nearest_railway_station_name":"karavadi railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":23.7,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Gangamma Temple, Dhenuvakonda), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"ae373d42-68a1-48b1-85a3-12d397551d5c","name":"Agatheeswarar Temple, Villivakkam","deity":"Agatheeswarar (Shiva)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Chennai","town":"Villivakkam","latitude":13.108,"longitude":80.209,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Cooum River","river_distance_km":3.2,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-08T06:51:40.218447+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Reported to be 800 years old or more, distinct from the similarly named Agatheeswarar temple in Nungambakkam.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Chennai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":13.5,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Anna Nagar West railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Contributor-submitted local survey","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"ae3a6664-29df-45a8-8644-a1b1573a5519","name":"Kailasanathar Temple, Kanchipuram","deity":"Kailasanathar (Shiva) / Uma","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Pallava monument"],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Kanchipuram","town":"Kanchipuram","latitude":12.8342,"longitude":79.6903,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Vegavathy","river_distance_km":1.5,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-08T06:23:03.123234+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:35.665826+00:00","sthala_purana":"Built in sandstone by the Pallava king Narasimhavarman II (Rajasimha) in the early 8th century and completed by his son Mahendravarman III, this is the oldest structural (built, not rock-cut) temple in Kanchipuram and one of the earliest surviving Dravidian-style stone temples anywhere. A narrow circumambulatory passage rings the main shrine, lined with 58 small sub-shrines originally holding stucco images of different forms of Shiva — many of the temple's sculptures still carry traces of their original fresco paintwork. Its architecture is considered a direct precursor to the later Kailasanatha temple built by the Rashtrakutas at Ellora.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"காஞ்சி கைலாசநாதர் கோயில்","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Chennai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":54.6,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Kanchipuram railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":2.2,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Historical/epigraphical record; ASI documentation","architecture_style":"Pallava","construction_century":8},{"id":"ae69f2f1-7dbe-4f77-b1f2-dd471db2804d","name":"Navalinga Temple","deity":"Shiva (nine lingas)","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Karnataka","district":"Koppal","town":"Mandaligere","latitude":15.45889,"longitude":75.96,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Hirehalla River","river_distance_km":11.9,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:38.196668+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A cluster of nine Hindu temples at Kukkanur in Koppal district, built in the 9th century under the Rashtrakuta king Amoghavarsha I or his son Krishna II. Built in the South Indian Dravida style, each temple has a Shiva linga, which gives the group its name, meaning nine lingas.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Jindal Vijaynagar Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":78.8,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Banni Koppa railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":6.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Navalinga Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Dravida","construction_century":9},{"id":"af8a73e7-ca50-4b18-ac81-a1b3b22e031f","name":"Ranchhodraiji Temple, Dakor","deity":"Krishna, worshipped as Ranchhodrai (Ranchhodraiji)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Gujarat","district":"Kheda","town":"Dakor","latitude":22.75443,"longitude":73.14898,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Mahi River","river_distance_km":11,"best_season_notes":"Sharad Purnima (the Ashwin/October full moon) is the temple's most important festival, drawing large numbers of pilgrims who walk to Dakor.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-22T08:50:17.345148+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Devotional tradition holds that the Ranchhodrai murti, linked to the Dwarkadhish Temple in Dwarka, came to Dakor with the devotee Bodana in 1151 CE — presented as legend rather than settled history. Simple worship is said to have continued for centuries until 1625, when the Pushtimargi gosvami Harirai established formal Vallabh-sampradaya rituals. The temple building that stands today, however, was constructed considerably later, by Gopal Tambvekar (Gopal Jagannath Ambekar), a Peshwa-court shroff from Poona, in 1769–70. It is enclosed by a fort wall near Gomti Lake, with 8 domes, 24 turrets, and a central dome rising about 27m, and Dakor is counted among the \"Pancha Dwarka\" pilgrimage sites.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Vadodara Green Airport Harni","nearest_airport_distance_km":47.2,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Dakor railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Devotional tradition (Bodana legend); temple construction records","architecture_style":"Maratha-era temple architecture (built 1769–70)","construction_century":18},{"id":"afd6e0c7-d411-4014-8f92-f53352f0e05b","name":"Sri Peddamma Thalli Temple","deity":"Peddamma","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Telangana","district":"Hyderabad","town":"Hyderabad","latitude":17.43065,"longitude":78.40499,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Musa River","river_distance_km":6.4,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:01.041713+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple at Jubilee Hills in Hyderabad, Telangana, dedicated to Peddamma, a village goddess form linked to the Vedic goddess Shachi. It is especially busy during the Bonalu festival season.","