here Krishna is worshipped as Madan Mohan, with Kali, Tara and Bhavani also honoured. It was founded in the late 19th century by Maharaja Nripendra Narayan of the Koch dynasty. Besides being a place of worship, it is a landmark in the history of the princely state.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Cooch Behar","nearest_airport_distance_km":2.7,"nearest_railway_station_name":"New Cooch Behar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":4.3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Madan Mohan temple, Cooch Behar), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":19},{"id":"b72ad1c3-0396-4b57-9edf-deac92c8b770","name":"Ulagalantha Perumal Temple, Kanchipuram","deity":"Ulagalantha Perumal (Vishnu, in his Trivikrama/Vamana form) / Amuthavalli","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Kanchipuram","town":"Kanchipuram","latitude":12.83917,"longitude":79.705,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Vegavathy","river_distance_km":1,"best_season_notes":"Nov–Feb.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-09T13:54:39.898029+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:35.665826+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of the 108 Divya Desams glorified in the Nalayira Divya Prabandham, believed to have been built by the Pallavas with later contributions from the Cholas, Vijayanagara kings, and Madurai Nayaks. Vishnu is worshipped here as Ulagalantha Perumal (\"the one who measured the worlds\"), commemorating his Trivikrama form in which he strode across the universe in three steps to reclaim it from the demon-king Mahabali.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Chennai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":53,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Kanchipuram East railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":0.8,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Nalayira Divya Prabandham; local/regional tradition","architecture_style":"Dravidian, Pallava origins","construction_century":null},{"id":"b756c615-c961-45f5-922b-7802c72b868b","name":"Punaura Dham","deity":"Sita (Janaki)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Bihar","district":"Sitamarhi","town":"Punaura","latitude":26.5865,"longitude":85.4668,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Bagmati River","river_distance_km":13.1,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:14.599574+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A pilgrimage temple at Punaura near Sitamarhi, Bihar, revered as the birthplace of Sita. It is among the few temples where she is worshipped on her own. It is part of the Government of India's Ramayana circuit and is listed as a cultural heritage site.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Muzzafarpur Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":54.3,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Sitamarhi railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Punaura Dham), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"b7656f1d-2295-460f-b4d9-ad4b4da91a84","name":"Kanaka Mahalakshmi Temple, Visakhapatnam","deity":"Kanaka Mahalakshmi (Lakshmi)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Andhra Pradesh","district":"Visakhapatnam","town":"Visakhapatnam (Burujupeta)","latitude":17.7006,"longitude":83.2961,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Meghadri Gedda River","river_distance_km":1.4,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb; Navaratri and the temple's own annual brahmotsavam draw the largest crowds.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-04T13:05:55.016033+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"The swayambhu (self-manifest) idol here is depicted with one arm missing. Local tradition holds the goddess herself severed it as part of a vow, granting prosperity only to the worthy and deliberately withholding it from the ungrateful — an alternate telling holds the incomplete form instead symbolises that her abundance can never be fully contained or depicted. Devotees describe her as the presiding deity of Visakhapatnam. By local record, the idol was recovered from a well in 1912 and installed at a small roadside shrine, then relocated a short distance in 1917 during road-widening.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"శ్రీ కనక మహాలక్ష్మి దేవస్థానం","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Alluri Sitarama Raju International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":37.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Visakhapatnam railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":2.4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"b786646d-e6c7-40e1-a46d-286d5842271a","name":"Adi Badri Temple","deity":"Vishnu","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":["Panch Badri"],"country":"India","state":"Uttarakhand","district":"Chamoli","town":"Adibadri","latitude":30.15,"longitude":79.25,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Ātā Gād River","river_distance_km":1.6,"best_season_notes":"Accessible year-round, being at a much lower elevation than the other Panch Badri shrines; reached from Karnaprayag, near Chandpur Garhi fort.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-05T08:35:20.91066+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A complex of sixteen small shrines, of which seven date to the Gupta period (5th–8th century CE), traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya. Considered the first among both the Panch Badri and the wider Sapta Badri circuit — tradition holds Vishnu resided here through the Satya, Treta, and Dvapara ages before relocating to Badrinath itself in the current Kali Yuga, hence the name \"Adi\" (first) Badri.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"आदि बद्री मंदिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Dehradun Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":102.8,"nearest_railway_station_name":null,"nearest_railway_distance_km":null,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition","architecture_style":"Gupta-period stone shrine complex","construction_century":6},{"id":"b7ae6568-369a-475b-92a7-e3a967989d29","name":"Dakshineswar Kali Temple, Kolkata","deity":"Kali (Bhavatarini)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"West Bengal","district":"North 24 Parganas","town":"Dakshineswar, Kolkata","latitude":22.6547,"longitude":88.3573,"sandhya_friendly":"yes","sandhya_notes":"The temple sits directly on the Hooghly (Ganga) riverbank.","samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Bhog Ghar — vegetarian meals available via a low-cost coupon system, not entirely free","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0.1,"nearest_river_name":"Hooghly (Ganga)","river_distance_km":0,"best_season_notes":"Dec–Feb; associated with Sri Ramakrishna Paramahamsa, who lived here. Kolkata's cyclone risk, like the rest of the West Bengal coast, is highest in Oct–Nov, ahead of a cooler, drier winter.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T11:30:32.947142+00:00","sthala_purana":"The goddess Bhavatarini is worshipped here, and the temple is inseparable from Sri Ramakrishna Paramahamsa, who served as its priest and had his realisations in its precincts.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"দক্ষিণেশ্বর কালীমন্দির","morning_opens_at":"06:00:00","morning_closes_at":"12:30:00","evening_opens_at":"15:00:00","evening_closes_at":"20:30:00","timings_notes":"Evening hours extend to roughly 9pm from April to September.","accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Netaji Subhas Chandra Bose International Airport, Kolkata","nearest_airport_distance_km":20,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Dakshineswar Railway Station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":null,"architecture_style":"Navaratna (nine-spire) style; the complex includes twelve riverside Shiva shrines, a Radha-Krishna temple and a bathing ghat","construction_century":19},{"id":"b7e80815-28e4-4400-8154-d45cc8a0e14f","name":"Buddhanath Temple","deity":"Shiva","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Odisha","district":"Khordha","town":"Balipatna","latitude":20.19668,"longitude":85.94849,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Prachi River","river_distance_km":5.6,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:38.196668+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A 12th-century Shiva temple in Garedi Panchana village, Balipatna block, about 22 km from Bhubaneswar. It is said to have been built by King Chodaganga Dev of the Somavanshi dynasty and to follow Tantric principles. According to local legend, snakebite victims brought to the premises do not die. It was recently renovated.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Biju Patnaik International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":15.4,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Bhubaneswar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":13.4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Buddhanath Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":12},{"id":"b80708b7-a0d5-4794-a8e1-e8c256a158f6","name":"Paat Baba Mandir","deity":"Hanuman","sampradaya":null,"significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Madhya Pradesh","district":"Jabalpur","town":"Bhita","latitude":23.17255,"longitude":79.97825,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadh