the Tunga is gentle and clean for ritual bathing in this window.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-05T19:02:16.315601+00:00","sthala_purana":"Adi Shankaracharya is said to have chosen this spot on the Tunga after seeing a cobra spread its hood to shade a frog in labour — predator sheltering prey, a sign of the place's sattvic character. He established the southern Amnaya peetham here and installed Sharada, whose mahavakya is Aham Brahmasmi.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"ಶೃಂಗೇರಿ ಶಾರದಾ ಪೀಠ","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Mangalore International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":75,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Karkala Railway Station","nearest_railway_distance_km":45,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":null,"architecture_style":"A blend of Dravidian, Vijayanagara, Hoysala, and Kerala styles across the matha complex","construction_century":14},{"id":"c3984986-dbc6-46d2-99e1-711e3c6cb5df","name":"Panchalingeshwara Temple, Govindanahalli","deity":"Shiva as Panchalingeshwara","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Karnataka","district":"Mandya","town":"Govindanahalli","latitude":12.7738,"longitude":76.3916,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Hemavati River","river_distance_km":3.3,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:38:38.196668+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A Hoysala temple at Govindanahalli, Mandya district, built around 1238 in the reign of King Vira Someshwara. The name means five lingas. The Archaeological Survey of India protects it as a monument of national importance, and the Hoysala sculptor Ruvari Mallitamma is known to have contributed to it.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Mysore Mandakalli Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":66.7,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Mandagere railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":5.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Panchalingeshwara Temple, Govindanahalli), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Hoysala","construction_century":13},{"id":"c3b0caa1-54f2-47f2-aa1a-f82cdf76c409","name":"Bhimashankar Jyotirlinga","deity":"Shiva (Bhimashankar)","sampradaya":null,"significance":["Jyotirlinga"],"country":"India","state":"Maharashtra","district":"Pune","town":"Bhimashankar","latitude":19.0728,"longitude":73.5514,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Temple Prasadalaya — free vegetarian meals","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0.3,"nearest_river_name":"Bhima (origin nearby)","river_distance_km":2,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb; the wildlife-sanctuary forest route is lush but slippery in monsoon.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-04T18:03:34.795958+00:00","sthala_purana":"Shiva is said to have taken form here to destroy the asura Tripurasura, and the sweat of that exertion is held to have become the Bhima river, which rises nearby.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"भीमाशंकर ज्योतिर्लिंग","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Pune Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":110,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Pune Junction","nearest_railway_distance_km":100,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Shiva Purana / Skanda Purana (Jyotirlinga tradition)","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"c40c0fe2-9ecd-448e-976a-625a8a169186","name":"Maa Dewri Temple, Deori","deity":"Maa Dewri (16-armed \"Solha Bhuji\" form of Durga/Devi)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Adivasi priesthood"],"country":"India","state":"Jharkhand","district":"Ranchi","town":"Deori","latitude":23.04611,"longitude":85.68278,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Kanchi Nadi River","river_distance_km":9.4,"best_season_notes":"Navratri is the major festival.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-22T08:39:44.697229+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Local tradition holds the temple was built around 1300 CE by Kera, a Munda (Adivasi) king, after the goddess reportedly helped him regain power following a military defeat — presented here as legend, since sources give an inconsistent range (some place the temple's origins as early as the 10th–12th century) rather than a settled date. The sanctum's roughly three-foot murti is locally said to be some 700 years old. Built from dry-stone construction — interlocking stones with no mortar — the temple is unusual in Jharkhand for still being served by Pahan (tribal) priests alongside a Brahmin priest tradition.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Ranchi Birsa Munda Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":47.5,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Tiraldih railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":27.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local tradition","architecture_style":"Ancient dry-stone construction (interlocking stones, no mortar)","construction_century":null},{"id":"c421a041-5155-409a-925b-a338a01c3c70","name":"Hikkal","deity":"Srinivasa (Hikkallappa)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Karnataka","district":"Tumakuru","town":"Hikkallu","latitude":13.33028,"longitude":77.24556,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":null,"river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:54.58588+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:00.624755+00:00","sthala_purana":"A hill shrine near Tumakuru, Karnataka, where Srinivasa is worshipped as a stone shaped like a broken anthill, with Sridevi and Bhudevi. A Balaji idol installed in 1999 came from the Tirumala devasthanam, which counts this among its sub-shrines.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Kempegowda International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":52,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Hirehalli railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":8.3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Hikkal), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"c44b0c2b-7e8a-4dae-bdfd-8ce35410938e","name":"Purasawalkam Gangadeeswarar Temple","deity":"Shiva as Gangadeeswarar with Pankajambal","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Tamil Nadu","district":"Chennai","town":"Chennai","latitude":13.08465,"longitude":80.25432,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Cooum River","river_distance_km":1.5,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:59:23.880063+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A five-tiered Shiva temple in Purasawalkam, Chennai, managed by the Tamil Nadu HR&CE Department. The goddess is Pankajambal. It is held to be the shrine of the 108th Shiva linga installed by Bhagirathan, and to have the same glory as Kashi Vishwanath. Kulothunga Chola II is said to have built and repaired it. It has a temple pond.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Chennai International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":13.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Chennai Egmore railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.1,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Purasawalkam Gangadeeswarar Temple), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"c474ef31-f8ad-4bc0-8fc8-3ecb5ca2cb78","name":"Manapullikavu","deity":"Bhagavathy","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Kerala","district":"Palakkad","town":"Palakkad","latitude":10.75806,"longitude":76.665,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Bharathapuzha","river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T10:59:23.880063+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:00.624755+00:00","sthala_purana":"A Bhagavathy temple in Palakkad city, Kerala, near the Palakkad-Chittur road. It was once privately owned by the Kenath family and is now run by the Malabar Devaswom Board. It is best known for its annual Vela festival, and tradition links its origins to the Mattappilly Bhadrakali Mana.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Coimbatore International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":51.6,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Palakkad Junction railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":5.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Manapullikavu), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"c4eca56f-cc27-4b84-8183-ded23f434b28","name":"Dashabhuja Ganapati Temple, Pune","deity":"Ganesha as Dashabhuja Ganapati","sampradaya":"Ganapatya","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Maharashtra","district":"Pune","town":"Pune","latitude":18.50578,"longitude":73.8258,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Mutha River","river_distance_km":1.1,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:44.755986+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A Ganesha temple in Pune that Sardar Haripant Phadke once owned before it passed to the Peshwas as a dowry gift. Thousands visit daily, more during Ganesh Chaturthi. The idol's trunk rests on its right side, which is held to be rarer and more sacred than the usual form.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Pune International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":12.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Shivajinagar railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":4,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Dashabhuja Ganapati Temple, Pune), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"c526b23e-ceb2-46c7-bfbc-3a7889eac0e8","name":"Shrinathji Temple, Nathdwara","deity":"Krishna (Shrinathji)","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Rajasthan","district":"Rajsamand","town":"Nathdwara","latitude":24.9333,"longitude":73.8167,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"pure_veg_available","food_source_name":"Chhappan Bhog prasad distributed to devotees after each darshan — peda, laddu, makhan mishri, panchamrit, and seasonal fruit","food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":0,"nearest_river_name":"Banas","river_distance_km":1,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Mar; a major Pushtimarg (Vallabhacharya sampradaya) pilgrimage site — darshan follows a fixed multi-times-daily schedule.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-03T11:51:56.898831+00:00","updated_at":"2026-09-05T18:23:31.36475+00:00","sthala_purana":"The deity, Shrinathji — Krishna as a seven-year-old lifting Govardhan Hill on one finger — is traditionally said to have travelled from Braj (near Mathura) in the late 17th century to escape Aurangzeb's campaign against temples there. According to the tradition, the deity's cart became stuck in the mud at Nathdwara and was taken as a sign to enshrine it on the spot. It remains the principal seat of the Pushtimarg, the devotional path founded by Vallabhacharya.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"श्रीनाथजी मन्दिर","morning_opens_at":"05:30:00","morning_closes_at":"12:15:00","evening_opens_at":"16:00:00","evening_closes_at":"19:30:00","timings_notes":"Darshan actually follows eight separate Jhanki (glimpse) windows through the day — Mangala, Shringar, Rajbhog, Sandhya, Shayan, and others — rather than one continuous block; the schedule shifts further around Janmashtami, Holi (Phool Phag), and Diwali.","accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Udaipur (Maharana Pratap) Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":48,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Nathdwara Railway Station","nearest_railway_distance_km":3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Local/regional tradition, not a single classical Sanskrit text","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"c58895cf-9eae-4111-aba7-1f5dfb2b3587","name":"Mahadev Temple, Tambdi Surla","deity":"Shiva as Mahadeva","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Goa","district":"Kushavati","town":"Tambdi Surla","latitude":15.43906,"longitude":74.25242,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Pasal River","river_distance_km":1.3,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:10:09.085477+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"A 13th-century Kadamba-style temple of Shiva in Goa that is still used for worship. It is a protected Monument of National Importance under the ASI, and the article compares it to the temples at Aihole in neighbouring Karnataka.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Goa International Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":45.5,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Castle Rock railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":9.8,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Mahadev Temple, Tambdi Surla), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":"Kadamba","construction_century":13},{"id":"c59ef793-fd93-4dc3-89f3-540881920f2a","name":"Jagannath Temple, Dhurwa (Ranchi)","deity":"Jagannath","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Jharkhand","district":"Ranchi","town":"Dhurwa","latitude":23.3169,"longitude":85.2817,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Subarnarekha River","river_distance_km":1.4,"best_season_notes":"Rath Yatra, with an annual fair in Ashadha, is the major festival.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-26T21:24:56.957301+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"Wikipedia records the temple as built by Thakur Ani Nath Shahdeo of Barkagarh and completed in December 1691; its claim that Aurangzeb desecrated it in the same year is questionable and not repeated here. The temple collapsed on 6 August 1990 and was rebuilt, reopening in February 1992, so the standing building is a 1990s reconstruction of the original.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":"Per a search summary, not an official schedule: roughly 5am–12pm and 3–6pm.","accessibility_notes":"The temple stands on a hillock reached by a staircase or a motorable path; the number of steps and wheelchair access are not verified.","nearest_airport_name":"Birsa Munda Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":6,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Ranchi Junction","nearest_railway_distance_km":11,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Jagannath Temple, Ranchi)","architecture_style":"Kalinga style, a smaller version of the Puri temple","construction_century":17},{"id":"c5d01942-d73b-4160-9e3e-d8819fa75711","name":"Harsiddhi Mata Temple, Ujjain","deity":"Harsiddhi (Devi)","sampradaya":"Shakta","significance":["Shakti Peetha"],"country":"India","state":"Madhya Pradesh","district":"Ujjain","town":"Ujjain","latitude":23.1815,"longitude":75.7695,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Kshipra","river_distance_km":1,"best_season_notes":"Oct–Feb; especially crowded during Navratri, when the temple's twin 51-foot deepstambhas (lamp towers) are lit with over a thousand oil lamps each.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-05T16:25:41.548561+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:00.624755+00:00","sthala_purana":"One of the Ashtadasha Shakti Peethas, standing near Rudra Sagar close to Mahakaleshwar. Tradition holds Sati's elbow fell here as Shiva carried her body across the cosmos. A separate legend credits King Vikramaditya with reviving the goddess's worship here after she helped him defeat a tyrant, hence \"Harsiddhi\" — the goddess \"who fulfills desires.\" The present structure was renovated in the Maratha period, with the distinctive twin lamp-towers added under the patronage of Maharani Ahilyabai Holkar.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":"हरसिद्धि माता मंदिर","morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Devi Ahilyabai Holkar Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":51.4,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Ujjain Junction railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":1.3,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Ashtadasha Shakti Peetha tradition; local/regional legend (Vikramaditya)","architecture_style":"Maratha-era renovation","construction_century":18},{"id":"c68beae3-65ed-4819-b306-997fb35c22f3","name":"Madan Mohan Temple, Karauli","deity":"Krishna as Madan Mohan","sampradaya":"Vaishnava","significance":[],"country":"India","state":"Rajasthan","district":"Karauli","town":"Sitapur","latitude":26.81842,"longitude":77.06047,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Bhadravati","river_distance_km":null,"best_season_notes":null,"submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-11-04T11:04:47.875457+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-04T12:15:00.624755+00:00","sthala_purana":"A temple in Karauli, Rajasthan, on the banks of the Bhadravati River, a tributary of the Banas, in the Aravalli hills within the Braj region. It is dedicated to Krishna as Madan Mohan, whose central image is flanked by Radha and Lalita.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":null,"accessibility_notes":null,"nearest_airport_name":"Agra Airport","nearest_airport_distance_km":95.9,"nearest_railway_station_name":"Hindaun City railway station","nearest_railway_distance_km":7.5,"emergency_contact_notes":null,"sthala_purana_source":"Wikipedia (Madan Mohan Temple, Karauli), summarised; location via OpenStreetMap","architecture_style":null,"construction_century":null},{"id":"c72ab68d-65e1-4068-aabb-b41bfccd63f9","name":"Kaal Bhairav Mandir, Varanasi","deity":"Kaal Bhairav — a fierce manifestation of Shiva, revered as the Kotwal (guardian/magistrate deity) of Kashi","sampradaya":"Shaiva","significance":["Kotwal of Kashi"],"country":"India","state":"Uttar Pradesh","district":"Varanasi","town":"Varanasi","latitude":25.31765,"longitude":82.97391,"sandhya_friendly":"unknown","sandhya_notes":null,"samidhadhanam_friendly":"unknown","samidhadhanam_notes":null,"food_tier":"unknown","food_source_name":null,"food_source_contact":null,"food_distance_km":null,"nearest_river_name":"Varuna River","river_distance_km":1.8,"best_season_notes":"Bhairav Ashtami is the major festival.","submitted_by":null,"status":"approved","verified_by":null,"moderator_note":null,"created_at":"2026-09-22T10:01:49.265512+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-05T02:38:03.965515+00:00","sthala_purana":"The exact founding date isn't documented; the current structure is dated to the mid-17th century. Tradition holds that no one may reside in, or even enter, Varanasi without Kaal Bhairav's sanction, and it's customary to visit here after Kashi Vishwanath darshan. This is a different temple from the Kal Bhairav Temple in Ujjain, Madhya Pradesh, already in this database — that one is known for liquor offerings to the deity, while this Varanasi temple's distinctive ritual is priests sweeping away bad omens with peacock-feather brooms and tying holy threads on devotees.","etiquette_notes":null,"native_name":null,"morning_opens_at":null,"morning_closes_at":null,"evening_opens_at":null,"evening_closes_at":null,"timings_notes":"Per tourism sources, not an official schedule: roughly 5am–1:30pm and 4:30–9:30pm, with a midday closure. Mangala Aarti around 4am, Bhog Aarti around 1pm, Sandhya Aarti around 8–8:30pm.","accessibility_notes":null,"